<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">New Sweden</h1><p class="st-meta">1638 CE – 1655 CE · capital Fort Christina</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Sweden's short-lived colony on the Delaware River, which introduced the log cabin to North America before falling to the Dutch in 1655.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> Company, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and Swedish investors, sent the ships <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-nyckel">Kalmar Nyckel</a> and Fogel Grip under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-minuit">Peter Minuit</a>, former director of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-netherland">New Netherland</a>. In 1638 they bought land from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a> and built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-christina">Fort Christina</a>, at what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilmington-delaware">Wilmington, Delaware</a>, named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christina-queen-of-sweden">Queen Christina</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The colony never had more than a few hundred settlers, many of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a> from the forests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-duchy-of-finland">Finland</a>, who brought the log cabin with them. Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johan-bjornsson-printz">Johan Printz</a>, in office from 1643 to 1653, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinicum-island">Tinicum Island</a>, built forts along the river and traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> and tobacco with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/susquehannock">Susquehannock</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1654 Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johan-risingh">Johan Risingh</a> seized the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-casimir">Fort Casimir</a>, provoking a response. In 1655 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-stuyvesant">Peter Stuyvesant</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-netherland">New Netherland</a> arrived with ships and soldiers, and New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> surrendered. The settlers stayed and kept their Lutheran churches, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloria-dei-old-swedes-church">Gloria Dei</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, under Dutch and later English rule.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/new-sweden.jpg" alt="New Sweden" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Original: Odengatan Vector: Kjoonlee · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Sweden_(1562%E2%80%931650).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1638 CE – 1655 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Fort Christina</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Peter Minuit, Johan Printz, Johan Risingh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheran Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swedish, Finnish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nya Sverige</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1646&amp;focus=new-sweden" aria-label="New Sweden on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2817 755 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where New Sweden lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3137 1011L5381 440M3137 1011L3186 980"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5381 440h0M3186 980h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3137 1011h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3137 1011h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Fort Christina. <a href="/atlas?year=1646&amp;focus=new-sweden">On the atlas &rarr;